% Introduction
% ============================================================
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:abi-intro}

The \emph{Application Binary Interface} (ABI) is the contract between separately
compiled program components.  It defines the calling convention (how arguments
are passed, how return values are returned), the memory layout of data types,
the system call interface, and the object file format.  ABIs are the invisible
infrastructure that makes it possible to link together code compiled by different
compilers, at different times, possibly in different languages.

Traditionally, ABIs are \emph{fixed}: once an ABI is defined for a platform, it
cannot be changed without breaking binary compatibility with existing compiled
code.  This rigidity has profound consequences:
\begin{itemize}
\item New features (e.g., new register usage, new calling conventions) cannot be
      adopted without a transition period.
\item Performance improvements that require ABI changes are delayed or abandoned.
\item Different components of a system may be compiled with different
      assumptions, leading to subtle incompatibilities.
\end{itemize}

\emph{Adaptive ABI negotiation} proposes a radical alternative: treat the ABI
not as a fixed contract but as a \emph{negotiation} between the caller and the
callee.  At each function call, the caller and callee discover each other's
capabilities and agree on a mutually beneficial calling convention.  This
enables:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Forward compatibility:} New calling conventions can be adopted
      incrementally, without breaking old code.
\item \textbf{Performance optimization:} Each call site can use the most
      efficient calling convention available.
\item \textbf{Feature detection:} Components can discover the capabilities of
      their partners and adapt accordingly.
\end{itemize}

This chapter develops the theory and practice of adaptive ABI negotiation,
from the conceptual framework (\cref{sec:abi-negotiation}) through runtime
selection (\cref{sec:abi-runtime}) and feature detection (\cref{sec:abi-feature})
to applications in library versioning and dynamic optimization
(\cref{sec:abi-applications}).

