% ABI as a Negotiation
% ============================================================
\section{ABI as a Negotiation}\label{sec:abi-negotiation}

\subsection{The Traditional ABI Model}\label{sec:abi-traditional}

In the traditional model, the ABI is a fixed specification:

\begin{definition}[Traditional ABI]\label{def:traditional-abi}
An \emph{ABI} is a specification $A$ that defines:
\begin{itemize}
\item The \emph{calling convention}: which registers are used for arguments,
      return values, and caller-saved/callee-saved values.
\item The \emph{data layout}: the size, alignment, and field offsets of data
      types.
\item The \emph{object format}: the format of object files, symbol tables, and
      relocation records.
\item The \emph{system call interface}: the mechanism for invoking OS services.
\end{itemize}
All compiled code must conform to $A$; code compiled with different ABIs cannot
be linked together.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Limitations of Fixed ABIs}\label{sec:abi-limitations}

Fixed ABIs create several problems:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Inflexibility:} Once deployed, the ABI cannot be changed.
      Performance improvements that require ABI changes must wait for the next
      platform generation.
\item \textbf{One-size-fits-all:} The same ABI is used for all call sites, even
      when different calling conventions would be more efficient.
\item \textbf{Version lock-in:} Libraries compiled with an old ABI cannot take
      advantage of new features.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{The Negotiation Model}\label{sec:abi-negotiation-model}

In the negotiation model, the ABI is a \emph{protocol} rather than a fixed
specification:

\begin{definition}[ABI Negotiation]\label{def:abi-negotiation}
\emph{ABI negotiation} is a protocol by which a caller $C$ and a callee $D$
agree on a calling convention at each call site.  The negotiation produces a
\emph{call convention} $cc$ that specifies:
\begin{itemize}
\item How arguments are passed (registers, stack, or a combination).
\item How return values are returned.
\item Which registers are caller-saved and callee-saved.
\item Any additional conventions (e.g., vector register usage, tag propagation).
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

