\section{Runtime ABI Selection}\label{sec:abi-runtime}

\subsection{Multi-Version Functions}\label{sec:abi-multiversion}

A \emph{multi-version function} is a function compiled in multiple variants, each
using a different calling convention.  At run time, the appropriate variant is
selected based on the caller's capabilities.

\begin{definition}[Multi-Version Function]\label{def:multiversion}
A \emph{multi-version function} $f$ is a set of implementations
$\{f_1, f_2, \ldots, f_n\}$ where each $f_i$ uses calling convention $cc_i$.
A \emph{dispatcher} selects the appropriate variant at each call site.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Multi-Version Function]\label{ex:multiversion}
Consider a function \texttt{dot\_product} compiled in three variants:
\begin{itemize}
\item $f_1$: Standard ABI (arguments on stack).
\item $f_2$: Extended ABI (arguments in registers, including vector registers).
\item $f_3$: Capability ABI (arguments as capabilities with bounds).
\end{itemize}
A caller using the standard ABI calls $f_1$; a caller using the extended ABI
calls $f_2$; and so on.
\end{example}

\subsection{Dispatch Mechanisms}\label{sec:abi-dispatch}

Several mechanisms can implement the dispatch:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Indirect calls through a dispatch table:} Each function has a
      table of entry points, one per calling convention.  The caller selects the
      appropriate entry point.
\item \textbf{PLT-based dispatch:} The Procedure Linkage Table (PLT) entry for
      the function performs the dispatch based on the caller's ABI version.
\item \textbf{Inline dispatch:} The compiler inlines the dispatch logic at each
      call site, using compile-time knowledge of the caller's ABI version.
\item \textbf{Tag-based dispatch:} The caller's ABI version is encoded in a tag
      (e.g., a special register or a thread-local variable) that the callee
      reads on entry.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Overhead of Dispatch}\label{sec:abi-overhead}

The overhead of ABI negotiation must be minimized:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Direct calls:} If the caller and callee are known at compile time
      to use the same ABI, no dispatch is needed.
\item \textbf{Cached dispatch:} The result of the first dispatch is cached, so
      subsequent calls to the same callee use the cached convention.
\item \textbf{Profile-guided dispatch:} The most common calling convention is
      placed first in the dispatch table, minimizing branch mispredictions.
\end{itemize}

