\section{Binary Compatibility}\label{sec:abi-compat}

\subsection{Compatibility Rules}\label{sec:abi-compat-rules}

For ABI negotiation to work, the system must maintain \emph{binary
compatibility}: old code must continue to work with new code.

\begin{definition}[Binary Compatibility]\label{def:binary-compat}
A new ABI version $A'$ is \emph{binary compatible} with an old version $A$ if:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Every calling convention in $A$ is also available in $A'$.
\item Every data layout in $A$ is also valid in $A'$.
\item Code compiled with $A$ can call code compiled with $A'$ and vice versa.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Extension, Not Modification}\label{sec:abi-extension}

The key principle for maintaining binary compatibility is \emph{extension, not
modification}: new ABI versions add new calling conventions and data layouts
without removing or changing existing ones.

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{New registers:} New ABI versions may use additional registers for
      argument passing, but must not change the usage of existing registers.
\item \textbf{New data types:} New ABI versions may add new data types, but
      must not change the layout of existing types.
\item \textbf{New conventions:} New ABI versions may add new conventions (e.g.,
      capability passing), but must support the old conventions.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Compatibility Matrices}\label{sec:abi-matrix}

A \emph{compatibility matrix} records which ABI versions can interoperate:

\begin{table}[htbp]
\caption{ABI Compatibility Matrix}\label{tab:abi-compat}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\hline
 & \textbf{v1} & \textbf{v2} & \textbf{v3} \\
\hline
\textbf{v1} & \checkmark & \checkmark & \checkmark \\
\textbf{v2} & \checkmark & \checkmark & \checkmark \\
\textbf{v3} & \checkmark & \checkmark & \checkmark \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

In this example, all versions are mutually compatible (each version supports
all older calling conventions).

% ============================================================
% Forward-Compatible ABIs
% ============================================================
\section{Forward-Compatible ABIs}\label{sec:abi-forward}

\subsection{Designing for the Future}\label{sec:abi-forward-design}

A \emph{forward-compatible} ABI is designed to accommodate future extensions
without breaking compatibility.  Key design principles include:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Reserved fields:} Include reserved fields in data structures that
      can be used for future extensions.
\item \textbf{Version tags:} Include version tags in function entry points and
      data structures, allowing the runtime to detect the version.
\item \textbf{Extensible calling conventions:} Define the calling convention in
      a way that allows new argument types and return conventions to be added.
\item \textbf{Negotiation protocols:} Include a negotiation protocol in the ABI
      itself, so that new versions can be discovered and used automatically.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{The Role of Metadata}\label{sec:abi-metadata}

ABI metadata---information about the calling convention, data layout, and
feature requirements attached to each function and data type---is essential for
adaptive negotiation:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Function metadata:} Records the calling conventions supported by
      each function variant.
\item \textbf{Type metadata:} Records the layout of each data type in each ABI
      version.
\item \textbf{Module metadata:} Records the ABI versions supported by each
      compiled module.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Negotiation Protocol}\label{sec:abi-protocol}

A simple negotiation protocol:

\begin{enumerate}
\item The caller sends its ABI version and feature set to the callee.
\item The callee selects the highest mutually supported version.
\item The callee returns its selected version.
\item The caller and callee use the negotiated convention for subsequent calls.
\end{enumerate}

This protocol can be implemented efficiently using a single register or a
thread-local variable to carry the version information.

