\subsection{Choosing and Caching an Exact ABI Descriptor}

A version number alone cannot describe the agreement between caller
and callee. Two implementations may both advertise a second version
but use different conventions for returning an aggregate. Conversely,
one implementation may offer an older descriptor as a distinct entry
point. Treat the offered choices as a set of exact, comparable
contracts rather than as a pair of integers to minimize.

\textbf{Contract.} Given a caller's ordered list of descriptors it can
marshal, a callee's map from descriptors to entry points, and the
process's usable feature set, choose the first fully supported
descriptor or report no agreement. Descriptors are canonical and
include calling convention, data layout, feature requirements, and
signature. The bootstrap query uses a fixed baseline ABI. Trusted
metadata faithfully describes the entries, and lookups use an
immutable snapshot of the callee generation.

\begin{sudocode}[Select an entry point and its marshaller]
\Let{(g,O)}{\Call{SnapshotOffers}{}}
\For{each caller descriptor $d$ in preference order}
  \To{Hardware presence alone need not mean OS support.}
  \If{$d.\mathrm{features}\not\subseteq\mathrm{usable}$}
    \State continue
  \EndIf
  \Let{entry}{\Call{ExactLookup}{O,d}}
  \If{$entry$ is present}
    \To{Keep the selected entry and marshaller together.}
    \State \Return $(g,entry,d.\mathrm{marshaller})$
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\State \Return no agreement
\end{sudocode}

\textbf{Trace.} A caller prefers \(v2\) with a two-lane return
in vector registers; it also offers \(v1\) with a scalar pair
returned in caller-owned storage. The callee offers a distinct
\(v2\) using a different aggregate layout and an exact \(v1\).
With vector registers usable, the attempted \(v2\) lookup still
fails and \(v1\) is selected. Calling the new entry merely because
its version matches would not implement the scalar-pair contract.

Whenever selection succeeds, the chosen marshaller and entry agree on
all layout fields and required features. An expected linear search in
the caller's preferences suffices with indexed offers and fixed-size
descriptors. A cached answer must be invalidated on a generation
change or feature change, and its old generation must remain alive
while any call is using it.

\Cref{fig:visual-115} contrasts a version-number match with an exact
descriptor match on the return convention.

