\section{Applications}\label{sec:abi-applications}

\subsection{Library Versioning}\label{sec:abi-versioning}

Adaptive ABI negotiation enables \emph{fine-grained library versioning}:
different versions of a library can coexist in the same process, and each call
site uses the most appropriate version.

\begin{example}[Library Versioning]\label{ex:lib-version}
A process loads two versions of a math library:
\begin{itemize}
\item \texttt{libmath-v1.so}: Compiled with the standard ABI.
\item \texttt{libmath-v2.so}: Compiled with an extended ABI that uses vector
      registers for floating-point arguments.
\end{itemize}
Call sites compiled with the extended ABI call \texttt{libmath-v2.so} using
vector registers; call sites compiled with the standard ABI call
\texttt{libmath-v1.so} using the standard convention.  Both versions coexist
in the same process.
\end{example}

\subsection{Dynamic Optimization}\label{sec:abi-dynamic-opt}

Adaptive ABI negotiation enables \emph{dynamic optimization}: the calling
convention can be changed at run time to adapt to changing conditions.

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Hot-path optimization:} If a call site is identified as hot
      (frequently executed), the system can recompile the caller and callee to
      use a more efficient calling convention.
\item \textbf{Context-sensitive optimization:} Different call sites can use
      different calling conventions based on their specific needs (e.g., a call
      inside a tight loop uses registers; a rare call uses the stack).
\item \textbf{Adaptive optimization:} As the program's behavior changes (e.g.,
      different inputs trigger different code paths), the calling conventions
      can be adapted.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Heterogeneous Systems}\label{sec:abi-heterogeneous}

In heterogeneous systems (CPU + GPU, big.LITTLE, accelerators), different
processing elements may have different ABIs.  Adaptive negotiation enables
seamless communication between heterogeneous components:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{CPU--GPU calls:} The calling convention for GPU offload is
      negotiated based on the data layout and transfer mechanism.
\item \textbf{big.LITTLE:} Calls between big and LITTLE cores may use different
      calling conventions optimized for each core's register file.
\item \textbf{Accelerator offload:} Calls to specialized accelerators (TPUs,
      FPGAs) use conventions optimized for the accelerator's interface.
\end{itemize}

\begin{insight}
Adaptive ABI negotiation represents a fundamental shift in how we think about
the boundary between compiled components.  Rather than a rigid, fixed contract,
the ABI becomes a flexible protocol that adapts to the capabilities and needs
of the communicating components.  This is analogous to the way that network
protocols negotiate parameters (e.g., TLS version negotiation, HTTP content
negotiation).  The result is a more flexible, more efficient, and more
forward-compatible system.
\end{insight}

\begin{historical}
The idea of multiple calling conventions within a single system is not new:
x86 has long supported multiple calling conventions (\texttt{cdecl},
\texttt{stdcall}, \texttt{fastcall}, \texttt{vectorcall}), and compilers like
GCC support function-specific calling conventions through attributes.  The
GNU IFUNC mechanism~\cite{gnu-ifunc} provides load-time dispatch.  More recent
work on \emph{multi-version functions} in LLVM and the \emph{capability ABI}
in CHERI~\cite{watson2020cheri} push toward more flexible, negotiable ABIs.
The concept of adaptive ABI negotiation as a unified framework is emerging from
these individual efforts.
\end{historical}

% ============================================================
% Summary
% ============================================================
\section{Summary and Further Reading}\label{sec:abi-summary}

Adaptive ABI negotiation transforms the ABI from a fixed contract into a
flexible protocol, enabling forward compatibility, performance optimization, and
heterogeneous computing.  By treating the calling convention as a negotiation
rather than a mandate, the system can adapt to the capabilities of each
component and the needs of each call site.

Key open challenges include:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Overhead:} Minimizing the cost of dispatch and negotiation.
\item \textbf{Complexity:} Managing the complexity of multiple calling
      conventions and ABI versions.
\item \textbf{Toolchain support:} Extending compilers, linkers, and debuggers
      to support adaptive ABIs.
\item \textbf{Standardization:} Developing standards for ABI negotiation
      protocols.
\item \textbf{Security:} Ensuring that ABI negotiation does not introduce
      security vulnerabilities (e.g., through type confusion between ABI
      versions).
\end{itemize}
